 "drivingQuestion": "Whose view does the mishna's mixing of the bloods follow?",
 "outcome": "not_dialectical",
 "outcomeNote": "the identification of which tanna holds which view is taken up in the next unit",
 "continuesQuestionOf": null,
 "segments": [
  {
   "id": "yoma.57b.12",
   "daf": "57b",
   "sentenceId": 11,
   "he": "עֵירָה דַּם הַפָּר לְתוֹךְ דַּם הַשָּׂעִיר. תְּנַן כְּמַאן דְּאָמַר: מְעָרְבִין לִקְרָנוֹת. דְּאִיתְּמַר: רַבִּי יֹאשִׁיָּה וְרַבִּי יוֹנָתָן, חַד אָמַר: מְעָרְבִין, וְחַד אָמַר: אֵין מְעָרְבִין.",
   "en": "§ The mishna states that after the High Priest sprinkled the blood toward the curtain «he poured the blood of the bull into the blood of the goat.» The Gemara comments: «We learned» this mishna «in accordance with» the opinion of «the one who said» that the High Priest «mixes» the two types of blood before placing them «on the corners» of the inner altar, and he does not present each one separately. «As it was stated:» The later tanna’im, «Rabbi Yoshiya and Rabbi Yonatan,» debated this issue. «One» of them «said:» The High Priest «mixes» the blood for the purpose of placing the blood on the altar’s corners, «and one» of them «said: He does not mix» the blood."
  }
 ],
